<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ormus</h1><p class="st-meta">1060 CE – 1622 CE (approx.) · capital Hormuz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The island kingdom of Hormuz, which controlled the mouth of the Gulf and its horse and spice trade until Persia and England expelled the Portuguese.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arab and Persian traditions say the kingdom was founded in the 11th century on the mainland near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minab">Minab</a> by a ruler from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. Around 1300, harried by Mongol-era raids, its king moved the capital to the barren island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Jarun</a>, which took the name Hormuz.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> lived by trade, taxing ships between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and shipping Arabian horses to India. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited it. Kings controlled ports on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalhat">Qalhat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a>, and paid tribute to stronger mainland powers. Water and food had to be brought to the island.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> attacked in 1507 and in 1515 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>, which built a fortress and controlled its customs. In 1622 an Anglo-Persian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> captured the island, ending the kingdom; trade moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ormus.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ormus" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: RETFrog · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ormus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1060 CE – 1622 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hormuz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Baha al-Din Ayaz, Turan Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a>, <a href="/countries/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, <a href="/countries/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Hormuz, Hormuz, Ormuz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1341&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ormus" aria-label="Kingdom of Ormus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6165 1184 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ormus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6485 1440L6318 1458M6485 1440L6134 1224M6485 1440L4773 1044M6485 1440L6281 1235M6485 1440L6145 1251"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6318 1458h0M6134 1224h0M4773 1044h0M6281 1235h0M6145 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6485 1440h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6485 1440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hormuz. <a href="/atlas?year=1341&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ormus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

